III. Colorado College’s Block Plan: One Course at a Time

The second component of this proposal is inspired by Colorado College’s distinctive Block Plan.

Under this model, students typically study one course intensively during a block lasting approximately three and a half weeks, before moving on to the next course.

This is a different organizing principle from the quarter system.

A quarter system determines the length of the academic term. A block system determines how students allocate their attention within that term.

Combining the two would require careful scheduling, but the principles are compatible.

1. Concentrated intellectual engagement

Consider a student studying calculus.

Under a conventional semester schedule, the student might attend calculus lectures several times per week while simultaneously completing assignments for four other courses.

Under a one-course-at-a-time model, the student could spend the entire academic block studying calculus, attending lectures, solving problems, receiving feedback, and working through progressively more difficult applications.

This concentration could make it easier to sustain attention and establish connections between concepts.

It could also enable instructors to organize learning around a continuous sequence of explanations, exercises, discussions, and assessments.

2. Mathematics becomes an immersive discipline

Mathematics illustrates the potential value of concentrated study.

Students often struggle when mathematical ideas accumulate faster than they can consolidate their understanding.

A block devoted exclusively to calculus could provide time for students to develop conceptual intuition, practice techniques, examine proofs, and apply mathematical principles to real problems.

Subsequent blocks could address linear algebra, probability, optimization, and differential equations.

These subjects could then be integrated into later courses in machine learning, physics, economics, and engineering.

The objective would not simply be to teach more mathematics in less time. It would be to give students a more coherent experience of mathematical learning.

Nevertheless, mathematical proficiency requires retention and repeated practice. Universities adopting the Block Plan should therefore include cumulative exercises and spaced review after students move on to subsequent subjects.

3. Laboratory science becomes more practical

The Block Plan could also be valuable for laboratory-based disciplines.

Students studying chemistry could concentrate on experimental design, laboratory procedures, data analysis, and scientific interpretation.

Biology students could dedicate sustained periods to fieldwork, microscopy, experimental methods, or computational biology.

Engineering students could spend an entire block constructing and testing a working prototype.

Concentrated study would make it easier to organize certain projects that are difficult to complete when students must repeatedly interrupt their work for unrelated classes.

However, some experiments require long observation periods, and some laboratory procedures depend on equipment availability or biological processes. Such courses would need schedules adapted to their scientific requirements.

4. Writing and the humanities benefit from immersion

The same principle applies to disciplines that depend on reading, interpretation, and extended argument.

A student studying political philosophy could spend an entire block examining major texts, comparing arguments, participating in seminars, and producing a substantial analytical essay.

A literature course could combine intensive reading with discussion and critical writing.

A history course could focus on primary sources, historiography, and the construction of evidence-based historical arguments.

This approach could allow students to develop a sustained relationship with a subject rather than treating it as one obligation among several competing deadlines.

5. One course does not mean one kind of learning

The Block Plan should not be interpreted as a requirement that students spend every hour listening to lectures.

An intensive course should incorporate different learning activities, including:

* Direct instruction.
* Independent reading.
* Problem-solving.
* Laboratory work.
* Group discussion.
* Practical projects.
* Individual feedback.
* Research and writing.
* Comprehensive assessment.

The objective is concentrated engagement with one subject, not monotonous repetition of one teaching method.

